          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Writer Lydia Davis observed that while <u>                </u> literary forms, such as the novel, are recognizable as such even as they evolve, there are path breaking “intergeneric” forms that might, for example, use elements of both fiction and essays to create something unclassifiable. The late-period pieces of James Tate arguably fit in this category, since they straddle the line between prose and poetry."
        }
